{% comment %}
  Robots meta for crawl control.

  The theme already emits <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}"> from
  layout/theme.liquid, and Shopify's canonical_url drops the query string, so
  filtered collection URLs already point back to the base collection. This adds
  the stronger signal where canonical alone is not enough.

  The rule:

  - Search results: always noindex. Never useful in an index, and infinitely
    generatable.
  - Two or more active filters: noindex. This is where the combinatorial
    explosion lives — six concerns x six ingredients x a price range is
    thousands of near-duplicate URLs.
  - Exactly one active filter: left indexable on purpose. Single-facet pages
    like "serums" or "vitamin C" are genuine landing pages for a beauty store
    and are the whole point of the concern/ingredient collections.
  - Unwritten stub pages: noindex until they have real content. Careers, Press,
    Store Locator and Track Order ship as placeholders, and an indexed empty
    page is a site-quality signal rather than a neutral one. Remove a handle
    from this list the moment that page is written.

  Note on robots.txt: crawl control here is deliberately done with meta robots
  rather than Disallow rules. A Disallow stops Google fetching the page at all,
  which means it never sees the canonical or the noindex above — and it would
  also block the single-facet pages we want indexed. Meta wins on this store.

  `follow` in every case, so link equity still flows to the products.

  Accepts: nothing. Reads the current request.

  Usage:
  {% render 'robots-meta' %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign robots_value = ''

  assign stub_pages = 'careers,press,store-locator,track-order' | split: ','

  if request.page_type == 'search'
    assign robots_value = 'noindex,follow'
  elsif request.page_type == 'page' and stub_pages contains page.handle
    assign robots_value = 'noindex,follow'
  elsif collection != blank
    assign active_filters = 0

    for filter in collection.filters
      case filter.type
        when 'price_range'
          if filter.min_value.value != blank or filter.max_value.value != blank
            assign active_filters = active_filters | plus: 1
          endif
        else
          if filter.active_values.size > 0
            assign active_filters = active_filters | plus: 1
          endif
      endcase
    endfor

    if active_filters > 1
      assign robots_value = 'noindex,follow'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if robots_value != blank -%}
  <meta name="robots" content="{{ robots_value }}">
{%- endif -%}
